.TH HUB 1 "August 2026" "0.1.0" "Gorilla Portable Network Hub"
.SH NAME
hub \- turn a laptop into the network and hand a folder to every device in the room
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hub
.br
.B hub serve
.I folder
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hub get
.I url
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hub sums
.I file
.br
.B hub doctor
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hub
creates a wifi network where none exists and hands a folder of files to every
device in range. It is built for rooms with no internet and no router.
.PP
With no arguments it opens a screen, which is the way in for most people. Every
command below also works on its own.
.PP
Files are served in pieces that can be asked for individually, so a device that
loses the signal carries on from where it stopped rather than starting the whole
file again.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve \fIfolder\fR
Hand out the files in a folder.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-name \fInetwork\fR
Create a wifi network with this name and serve over it. Needs administrator
rights. Without it, the files go out over whatever network already exists.
.TP
.B \-\-password \fIword\fR
Password for that network, at least 8 characters. Required with \fB\-\-name\fR:
an open network lets anyone in range reach this computer, so one is never made.
.TP
.B \-\-helpers \fInumber\fR
How many devices to serve at once. Defaults to eight per processor.
.TP
.B \-\-port \fInumber\fR
Which port to listen on. Default 8080.
.RE
.TP
.B get \fIurl\fR
Download a file, resuming if it was interrupted. Run the same command again
after an interruption and it continues from where it stopped.
.RS
.TP
.B \-n \fInumber\fR
Parallel connections. Default 4, which was measured as the best on wifi; more
is slower and noisier, because the ceiling is airtime rather than threads.
.TP
.B \-o \fIfile\fR
Where to save it.
.TP
.B \-\-verify on
Check every piece against the server's fingerprint list.
.RE
.TP
.B sums \fIfile\fR
Fingerprint each piece of a file, using every processor. Put the result beside
the file as \fIfile\fR.sums and anyone downloading it can check what they get.
.TP
.B doctor
Say what this computer can and cannot do: processors, window size, wifi adapter,
addresses. Read this out when something will not work and there is nobody nearby
to ask.
.SH THE NETWORK IS PUT BACK
Creating a network replaces this computer's own wifi for as long as it runs. The
previous connection is restored on exit, and also by a systemd timer that is
pushed back every minute while the program is alive. If the program is killed
outright the wifi still comes back, within three minutes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Hand out a folder over a new network:
.PP
.RS
hub serve ~/lessons \-\-name Classroom \-\-password chalkdust
.RE
.PP
Fetch a file from the teacher's machine:
.PP
.RS
hub get http://10.42.0.1:8080/lessons.zip
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I file.parts
Records which pieces have arrived, so an interrupted download can continue.
Removed when the file is complete.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation, in plain language and in detail, is in
.IR /usr/share/doc/gorilla-portable-network-hub/ .
